---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BodySurfaceAreaCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/BodySurfaceAreaCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="body-surface-area-calculator">
  <BodySurfaceAreaCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the body surface area calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The body surface area calculator estimates the area of your skin in square metres (m²) from height and
      weight. It gives the result of six published formulas, so you can see how much they differ. In each
      formula <code>H</code> is height in centimetres and <code>W</code> is weight in kilograms.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mosteller (1987): <code>BSA = √(H × W ÷ 3600)</code></li>
      <li>Du Bois and Du Bois (1916): <code>BSA = 0.007184 × W^0.425 × H^0.725</code></li>
      <li>Haycock (1978): <code>BSA = 0.024265 × W^0.5378 × H^0.3964</code></li>
      <li>Gehan and George (1970): <code>BSA = 0.0235 × W^0.51456 × H^0.42246</code></li>
      <li>Boyd (1935): <code>BSA = 0.0003207 × H^0.3 × G^(0.7285 − 0.0188 × log10 G)</code>, with <code>G</code> the weight in grams</li>
      <li>Fujimoto (1968): <code>BSA = 0.008883 × W^0.444 × H^0.663</code></li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      For 170 cm and 70 kg, Mosteller gives √(170 × 70 ÷ 3600) = √3.3056 = 1.82 m². Du Bois gives 1.81 m²,
      Haycock 1.83 m², Gehan and George 1.83 m², Boyd 1.83 m² and Fujimoto 1.76 m². The six results span 1.76
      to 1.83 m².
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Each formula was fitted on a small group of people. The formulas agree well for adults of usual build and
      differ more for children and for very heavy or very light bodies. Fujimoto was fitted on Japanese adults.
      Drug doses are often scaled to body surface area; for a dose, use the value from your care team.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
